# Revert means git

When Ron asks to **revert**, **undo**, or **cleanly revert** a change,
he means a **clean git-based restore** of the files (or commit) in
question — not a manual rewrite that approximates the old content.

## Do

- Identify the last good tree (prior commit, `HEAD`, or the commit
  before the bad change).
- Restore with git, e.g. `git checkout <good> -- <paths>`,
  `git restore --source=<good> -- <paths>`, or `git revert <commit>`
  when undoing a committed change on the branch.
- Then re-check status / diff so the working tree matches that
  source for those paths.
- Update issue / roadmap notes only if the revert reopens work
  (e.g. leave `R*` open again).

## Do not

- Hand-edit files to “look like” the old version (`StrReplace` /
  rewrite as a fake undo).
- Leave partial leftovers from the bad change.
- Use destructive git (`reset --hard`, force push) unless Ron
  explicitly asks for that form of revert.
